 in jumps one  possibility would be that we have a  region where the system is not moving  this means zero matter waves here we  wonder boy waves here too and here free  but a little bit more detailed  consideration tells us that instead of  forming this kind of ring shaped  geometry it rather the system harder  breaks up into little whirlpools little  vortices and around each vortices we  have one full deployed wavelength now  this is a very Universal consequence if  we have matter which is a single wave  and indeed there's a very important  experiment on twenty years ago at  Berkeley where this phenomenon was  demonstrated with liquid helium and also  with post condensates recently a group  in Paris showed this effect I'm talking  about recent work so I want to talk now  about things which were done in our  laboratory less than a year ago so we  try to sort of go further and study  further aspects of it so what we did is  and this gives you sort of a taste what  happens in building 26 it was this tiny  little cloud in a big vacuum chamber and  then we use a laser beam and we rotate  the laser beam around the cloud and with  the laser beam we are spinning up the  gas and we are setting the gas in  rotation and then if you let the gas go  if you let the cloud ballistically  expand we can now compare the cloud  without rotation it's just the blob  which expands as I've shown you before  but with rotation  we observe those whirlpools so this is  again a very clear manifestation of the  wave nature of matter ordinary standard  substances would not behave like this  and again it's the most direct  observation it's a cloud which rotates  you let it expand you take a picture and  this is a shadow the shadow of this  cloud actually this is amethyst  emulation by one of my colleagues which  shows that this structure of whirlpools  is preserved upon expansion of the gas  we can't directly photograph the cloud  while it is rotating because the  structures are too small but by simply  switching off the magnetic drive and  everything expands we get almost we get  a magnification factor almost 4/3 and  the faster we rotated the cloud the more  vortices we observed so this is an  example of a vortex lattice which is  perfectly irregular this is just a more  fancy representation but let's just look  at this very regular vortex lattice with  those lines which guide the eye we see  how perfect nature is but it's not  always perfect I know if you look  carefully on the left hand side you see  a lattice defect you see a dislocation  and extra row of  vortices so we have now a system it's a  very dilute claw gas and we can now  study the properties of what is he study  the dynamics and there's a lot of  interest in doing that but finally let  me talk about something which is even  more recent and some work which is  currently in focus and this is ok I will  tell you in a moment this is related to  optical trapping I told you that our  workhorse is a magnetic container but so  well  magnetic containers are nice they've  allowed us to do all this work but  they've also the limitations because if  you want to move magnets you may have to  move all the magnets around you can only  keep atoms in spin up States and not  spin down states so there were many  reasons to  put those most condensates into other  containers and this is a container which  is not using magnetic field but electric  fields and it's the electric field of a  focused laser beam we call it optical  tweezers because it's really like it's  like a laser pointer we bring the light  to a focus and this focus sucks atoms  into it just by electric forces so a  couple of years ago we showed that we  can transfer condensate from the  magnetic trap into an optical trap but  our piece of pride and our latest  accomplishment is that we are now able  to use this optical trap as a transport  mechanism for post condensate  so by taking not really the laser part a  lens and translating it we can move the  focus by 40 centimeters so our group is  now the unique ability to form a  condensate in one vacuum chamber then  focus laser light on it and translate  the focus by about 40 centimeters and  carry the atoms in a new chamber so we  are now able to deliver condensate with  precision through pinholes and put  condensates into whole new environments  close to surfaces into micro traps in to  resonators and there's enormous  excitement and let me just show you one  of the results this is 40 centimeters  away from where we produce the  condensate and here it is and it is less  than a millimeter away from a surface  and we think there's a lot of future of  atomic physics in that some people dream  that we can take atoms guided metal  waves on on atom chips tiny patterns of  wires on a surface and we guide the  atoms around like in an ordinary chip  electrons are guided and this may allow  us to have new geometry is to find new  properties of quantum systems but it may  also allow us to build precision sensors  for rotation  but I think this example also for the  last week Sounders demonstrates some of  my surprises when we were hunting for  the post condensate we thought we would  be so happy if you would just see it if  you would get there but now we can take  condensates transport them over 40  centimeters we can keep them for a  minute and do experiments we can spin  them at high rotation and see those  vortices  so the system is enormous ly robust and  allows to branch out in a large variety  of studies so for me the post condensate  is now an new laboratory to do all sorts  of physics to do condensed metaphysics  vortex nucleation sound densities which  i extremely know that it's very easy for  the Yogi's to calculate the effect and  what I've done here is it's more for the  experts I've just listed major  developments in the field which have  happened just this year and there are  other exciting things and I think  there's more to come  so it's a privilege for scientists to be  in the middle of something like this to  be able to contribute to the basic  concepts and see the scores of a field  and see how its branching out actually I  don't have a slide for that but one  question which almost everybody asked me  what is the application what will come  out of it now  my take on that is twofold one is it's  fundamental research and we do  fundamental research to learn about  properties of nature to find properties  of metal ultra low temperatures to  understand microscopic window mechanics  on the other hand what we are doing here  is we are learning how to manipulate  atoms the building blocks of nature with  unprecedented precision we have quantum  control over the atoms we control their  wave properties and this unprecedented  control over atoms may lead to precision  measurements may contribute to  nanotechnology or let me speculate even  further may find uses in quantum  computation but this is  The Horizon and collaborators and other  groups are exploring that and future  will tell what is possible but if I look  backwards usually if something basic is  done our imagination is not sufficient  to predict what it is good for so  finally let me acknowledge the people I  was really privileged to work with I  think the best team of students and  postdocs in the whole world this lists  the people who have left the group and  contributed to the work but here's also  a long list of people who are currently  carrying out this exciting work usually  I show a group photo but I realized we  haven't taken a group photo recently so  we are just asked the people who are  working with me just to stand up and  it's a live group photo and they're  carrying out this work and ladies  thank you  enjoy  well that was fantastic Wolfgang you  lived up to expectations are there any  questions okay it's just fine  I don't think there have to be any  question we have a reception in the marl  our lounge I just want to add on behalf  of the physics department of course this  is an unbelievably exciting occasion  we've had five Nobel Prize winners in  the last 30 years as I told my  colleagues I'd like the rate to go a  little higher but I cannot imagine a  more wonderful person to receive the  prize than someone who not only has this  passion for science but can convey it so  beautifully so let's end just by  thanking Wolfgang one more time  you
 

